<section class="ip-section" id="custody-flow" aria-labelledby="custody-flow-heading">
  <div class="ip-section-heading">
    <h2 id="custody-flow-heading">Follow each item from approval to closure</h2>
    <p>Xinyinhai's physical controls keep customer projects separate and assign responsibility for controlled items. Making an item, moving it or finishing the project does not remove it from management.</p>
  </div>
  <ol class="ip-flow">
    <li><h3>Approve production</h3><p>Confirm the item, project purpose, authorized quantity and responsible person before making or commissioning it.</p></li>
    <li><h3>Identify the item</h3><p>Assign a controlled reference and record its project, type, quantity and status so similar items from different projects remain distinguishable.</p></li>
    <li><h3>Assign custody</h3><p>Record the custodian and storage location. Keep controlled items in the designated area under the applicable access rules.</p></li>
    <li><h3>Release or send out</h3><p>Authorize the recipient, purpose, quantity and destination. Record the handover, including an approved outside party when external work is required.</p></li>
    <li><h3>Return and check</h3><p>Record the returned quantity, identity and condition. Compare the return with the release record and investigate any difference.</p></li>
    <li><h3>Reconcile and close</h3><p>Confirm the agreed return, retention, further authorized transfer or disposal, and close the record after outstanding movements are resolved.</p></li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="ip-section" id="custody-record" aria-labelledby="custody-record-heading">
  <div class="ip-section-heading">
    <h2 id="custody-record-heading">A record connects the item, its keeper and its destination</h2>
    <p>Each movement is linked to the project and the people responsible for release and receipt. A custody record connects the following fields so an item can be traced through each approved movement.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="ip-split">
    <dl class="ip-record">
      <div><dt>Project and item reference</dt><dd>Project identifier and controlled item number.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Item description</dt><dd>Item type, applicable revision and identifying details.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Quantity and condition</dt><dd>Recorded quantities and condition at each movement.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Custodian and location</dt><dd>Responsible person and current approved storage location.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Authorized movement</dt><dd>Approval, purpose, sender, recipient, destination and handover date.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Return or final disposition</dt><dd>Return, approved retention or transfer, or authorized disposal and closure record.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <div>
      <h3>What stays within custody control</h3>
      <ul class="ip-checklist">
        <li>Moulds, tooling, prototypes and trial-moulded parts.</li>
        <li>Development samples, approval samples and sensitive parts or assemblies.</li>
        <li>Project-specific packaging, printed materials and other controlled product content.</li>
        <li>Surplus materials, rejected pieces, defective units and scrap that could still reveal the project.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Custody responsibilities and transfer records continue when work moves between engineering, production, quality and an approved external party. Items remain distinguishable by project throughout these movements.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="ip-section" id="reconciliation" aria-labelledby="reconciliation-heading">
  <div class="ip-section-heading">
    <h2 id="reconciliation-heading">Reconcile quantities and agree the final disposition</h2>
    <p>Items do not become unrestricted because they are no longer required, fail inspection or remain after a project ends. Their quantity, location and authorized next step still need to be accounted for.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="ip-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Controlled disposition of samples, tooling and surplus items">
    <table class="ip-table">
      <caption>The final disposition follows the customer-approved project arrangements.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Disposition</th><th scope="col">Control before closure</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Return</th><td>Confirm the authorized recipient, item references and quantities. Record the handover and receipt, including tooling and associated technical information where agreed.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Retain for an agreed purpose</th><td>Record the approved reason, responsible custodian, location and retention arrangements. Continued storage does not authorize use for another customer.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Transfer for further work</th><td>Approve the task and receiving party, record quantities and destination, and keep the return or next disposition open until resolved.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Scrap or destroy</th><td>Obtain the applicable approval, reconcile the quantity, control the disposal route and retain the agreed disposal or destruction record.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Quantities made or received are compared with the records for issue, return, approved retention, transfer and disposal. A missing item or unexplained difference is investigated before closure through the <a class="ip-text-link" href="/customization/ip-protection/confidentiality/#incident-response">project exception response process</a>.</p>
</section>
